Feature: A generated project reads each setting from the file its guide names
  As a developer working on a project generated by the toolbox
  I want development values in .env.dev and the real values of a deployed environment in .env
  So that what the guide tells me and what task does are the same thing

  # ONE freshly generated project and ONE live terminal. The guide every
  # generated project ships lists the three settings files in the order task
  # reads them. The next two cards follow it with a real setting and show which
  # value task really uses.
  #
  # ONE sentence = ONE card = ONE pixel baseline, asserted inside the step
  # (tolerance: 0). Every card is twinned with a check of the same fact: the
  # guide's text, the files git sees changed, and the value task prints when it
  # runs off camera.
  @env-layering
  Scenario: A development value in .env.dev wins over the default in .env.dist, and a .env file wins over both
    # Note: A project freshly generated by the toolbox, with every default answer. Section 12.1 of its guide, docs/guidelines/DevSecOps.md, lists the three settings files in the order task reads them, .env, then .env.dev, then .env.dist, and says "The first file that sets a variable wins."
    # Note: Its table says .env holds the real values of a deployed environment and git ignores it. Section 12.2 says that for development "There is nothing to copy or create."
    # Copy: grep -A 18 "^### 12.1" docs/guidelines/DevSecOps.md
    Given the project's guide lists its three settings files in the order task reads them
    # Note: The developer adds a setting, TASK_APP_URL, the way the guide says: its default, https://app.example.com, at the end of .env.dist, and its development value, http://localhost:8080, at the end of .env.dev. A small task in project/Taskfile.yml prints it.
    # Note: Below the diff, task project:monitor:app:info prints "The app runs at http://localhost:8080". That is the value from .env.dev.
    # Copy: git diff && task project:monitor:app:info
    When the developer adds a setting with a default in .env.dist and a development value in .env.dev, task uses the development value
    # Note: The same project, set up the way a staging server would be: a .env file with the real value, https://staging.example.com. git status lists it with "!!", which means git ignores it, so it can never be committed.
    # Note: grep shows the setting in the three files, in the order task reads them. task now prints "The app runs at https://staging.example.com": .env wins over both.
    # Copy: echo "TASK_APP_URL=https://staging.example.com" > .env && git status --short --ignored && grep TASK_APP_URL .env .env.dev .env.dist && task project:monitor:app:info
    Then a .env file, which git ignores, wins over both
